VM102 (entraconnect01) was never actually domain-joined, contradicting verified-claims.md:44.

SYMPTOM CHAIN (all via qm guest exec 102 unless noted):

1. Console login as Administrator with the freshly-rotated DOMAIN password failed. Initially
   misdiagnosed by Claude as the Secure Admin WS deny rule "working as intended" on a member
   server — that explanation was WRONG and is retracted; a machine not joined to the domain
   cannot process a domain GPO at all.

2. nltest /sc_verify:district.local
   { "err-data": "I_NetLogonControl failed: Status = 1722 0x6ba RPC_S_SERVER_UNAVAILABLE",
     "exitcode": 1, "exited": 1,
     "out-data": "ComputerName dc01.district.local  TcpTestSucceeded True" }
   Raw TCP/389 to DC01 succeeds; the secure channel does not. Distinct failures.

3. Get-Service Netlogon -> Status: Stopped, StartType: Manual
   Start-Service Netlogon -> FAILED (StartServiceFailed). StartType did flip to Automatic;
   Status remained Stopped. Dependency LanmanWorkstation was Running — not the blocker.

4. System event log, last 10 minutes, all Error/Warning/Critical:
   ProviderName: NETLOGON, Id: 3095, Level: Error
   "This computer is configured as a member of a workgroup, not as a member of a domain.
    The Netlogon service does not need to run in this configuration."
   (Two occurrences, 8:11:24 and 8:11:52 — both from today's start attempts. No older Netlogon
    events exist in the current 500-event retention window, so the moment the machine left/never
    entered the domain is NOT established by any capture.)

   Same window also shows, not yet chased:
   - "The Microsoft Azure AD Sync service terminated with the following service-specific error:
      Unspecified error" (Id 7024), repeating every few minutes
   - NtpClient unable to resolve time.windows.com
   - WinRM not listening

5. AUTHORITATIVE CONFIRMATION — machine side:
   (Get-CimInstance Win32_ComputerSystem) | Select-Object Domain,PartOfDomain,Workgroup,Name
   { "exitcode": 0, "exited": 1, "out-data":
     "Domain: DISTRICT.LOCAL   PartOfDomain: False   Workgroup: DISTRICT.LOCAL   Name: ENTRACONNECT01" }

   PartOfDomain: False. Note the trap: when PartOfDomain is False, Win32_ComputerSystem's
   Domain property reports the WORKGROUP name, not a domain. The workgroup here happens to be
   named the same string as the domain, so a glance at "Domain: DISTRICT.LOCAL" looks correct
   and is not.

6. AUTHORITATIVE CONFIRMATION — directory side (qm guest exec 100, against DC01):
   Get-ADComputer -Identity ENTRACONNECT01 ...
   { "err-data": "Cannot find an object with identity: 'ENTRACONNECT01' under: 'DC=district,DC=local'",
     "exitcode": 1, "exited": 1 }

   Get-ADComputer -Filter {Name -like "*entra*"} -Properties whenCreated,whenChanged,DistinguishedName
   { "exitcode": 0, "exited": 1 }   <- exit 0, zero objects returned. Clean negative, not an error.

   No computer object exists for this machine under any name. Both sides agree.

   (One failed intermediate attempt worth keeping: an earlier version of the -Filter query used
    escaped inner quotes inside a bash single-quoted string and died with
    "A positional parameter cannot be found that accepts argument '*entra*\'" — Claude's own
    bash-quoting bug, the same class of error hit twice already this session.)

WHERE THE ORIGINAL CLAIM CAME FROM: the member-server-build report records post-join Server
Manager showing "Workgroup: DISTRICT.LOCAL" and reads it as domain membership. Server Manager
labels that field "Domain:" when a machine IS joined and "Workgroup:" when it is not. The
screenshot was VM102 reporting it was not joined. That report also explicitly flags its GUI
screenshots as "informational, not filed evidence" — this is precisely the case where that
caveat mattered and was overlooked anyway. No qm guest exec capture of domain membership was
ever taken at build time.

LIKELY DOWNSTREAM IMPACT, NOT PROVEN: the entra-connect-install exercise tested five hypotheses
against the wizard error "The domain specified in the credentials does not exist or cannot be
contacted" and resolved none of them. A machine with working DNS/LDAP/GC reachability but no
actual domain membership is the most parsimonious explanation now available for that error.
Nothing captured isolates it as THE cause, and the wizard has not been re-run since — so this
is a strong candidate, not a settled finding.

